Implementation and Performance Notes: Hospital Records Management System

1\. Architectural Overview

The Hospital Records Management System is an Oracle PL/SQL application designed to manage patient records, doctor information, appointments, and medical treatments. It follows a modular programming approach using stored procedures, functions, explicit and implicit cursors, collections, and bulk DML operations.

2\. Key Concepts Implemented

Records (%ROWTYPE and User-Defined Records):

%ROWTYPE is used in procedures such as register_patient_proc and list_doctor_appointments to store complete table rows in PL/SQL variables. User-defined records are used when a task requires a custom combination of fields.

Collections (Nested Tables):

Nested tables, such as TYPE t_treatment_ids IS TABLE OF ..., are used in apply_bulk_treatment_adjustment to store lists of treatment IDs and related values in memory for batch processing.

Cursors:

Implicit Cursors:

Implicit cursors are automatically generated for INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE statements. Attributes such as SQL%FOUND and SQL%ROWCOUNT are used to determine whether rows were affected and how many records were processed.

Explicit Cursors:

Explicit cursors are used to process multiple query results in list_doctor_appointments through OPEN, FETCH, and CLOSE operations.

Bulk Processing and Performance Considerations:

BULK COLLECT:

BULK COLLECT retrieves multiple rows from the treatments table into PL/SQL collections in a single operation. This reduces the number of context switches between the SQL and PL/SQL engines compared to fetching records one at a time.

FORALL:

FORALL performs batch DML operations on treatment costs. It reduces context-switching overhead compared to executing separate DML statements for each record. The actual performance improvement depends on the amount of data being processed and the execution environment.

SAVE EXCEPTIONS:

SAVE EXCEPTIONS allows FORALL to continue processing other collection elements even when individual DML operations fail. Errors are captured and handled using SQL%BULK_EXCEPTIONS, including the ORA-24381 exception.

Exception Handling:

The application uses predefined exceptions and custom application errors raised through RAISE_APPLICATION_ERROR with error codes ranging from -20001 to -20004. It also includes safe cursor cleanup checks, such as:

IF c_appointments%ISOPEN THEN\
&#x20;   CLOSE c_appointments;\
END IF;

3\. Performance Summary

Bulk processing reduces repeated communication between the PL/SQL and SQL engines, which can improve application efficiency when handling large numbers of records.

BULK COLLECT and FORALL are used to process data in batches instead of relying entirely on row-by-row operations. These techniques help improve performance, reduce context-switching overhead, and make database operations more efficient.

However, actual performance improvements should be evaluated through testing with representative data volumes and different test cases.
